    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q55-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f$ has domain $\\mathbb{R}$, defined by $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0$ belongs to the stated domain, but $f(0)$ is undefined.",
          "worked": "$0\\in\\mathbb{R}$, but substituting $x=0$ gives $\\frac{1}{0}$, which is undefined. The rule does not assign an output to every domain element.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q55-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f$ has domain $(-\\infty,0]$, defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Negative inputs belong to the stated domain, but their square roots are not real.",
          "worked": "$-1\\in(-\\infty,0]$, but $\\sqrt{-1}$ is not real. Thus the rule does not assign a real output to every domain element.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q55-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f$ has domain $\\mathbb{Z}$, defined by $f(a+b)=a$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2=1+1=2+0$, but the rule gives $f(2)=1$ and $f(2)=2$. The same input has different outputs.",
          "worked": "The input $2$ can be written as $1+1$, $2+0$ or $0+2$. Applying the proposed rule to these representations gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(2)&=f(1+1)=1\\\\f(2)&=f(2+0)=2\\\\f(2)&=f(0+2)=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nEqual inputs receive different outputs, so the rule is not well-defined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q55-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f$ has domain $\\mathbb{Q}$, defined by $f\\left(\\frac{p}{q}\\right)=pq$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{2}{4}$, but the rule gives outputs $2$ and $8$. Equal inputs have different outputs.",
          "worked": "The fractions $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{2}{4}$ represent the same rational input. The proposed rule gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)&=1\\times2=2\\\\f\\left(\\frac{2}{4}\\right)&=2\\times4=8\\end{align*}$$\n\nEqual inputs receive different outputs, so the rule is not well-defined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q55-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\pm\\sqrt{x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $x=1$, the rule gives both $1$ and $-1$. A function must have exactly one output for each input.",
          "worked": "At $x=1$, the rule assigns $+\\sqrt{1}=1$ and $-\\sqrt{1}=-1$. This gives two different outputs for the same input, contradicting the definition of a function.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
